---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'

const BASE = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, '/')
// Each cell: [text, tone]. Tone 'yes' or 'no' colors the cell; facts only, no scores.
type Cell = string | [string, 'yes' | 'no']
const ROWS: [string, Cell, Cell, Cell][] = [
  ['Look', 'Seven styles: soft characters, gummy critters, glowing orbs and initials', '30+ styles, from pixel art to illustrated people', 'Six abstract variants: marble, beam, pixel, sunset, ring, bauhaus'],
  ['Framework components', ['React, Vue, Svelte, Angular and a Web Component', 'yes'], 'A JavaScript library: you place its SVG yourself', 'React'],
  ['Runtime dependencies', ['0', 'yes'], '0 in @dicebear/core; each style is its own package', '0 (React as a peer)'],
  ['Output', 'SVG string; PNG in the browser with toPng', 'SVG; PNG and JPEG with @dicebear/converter or the HTTP API', 'SVG as a React element'],
  ['Hosted HTTP API', ['No', 'no'], ['Yes, free', 'yes'], 'A separate paid service'],
  ['Photo with fallback', ['Built in: src shows once it loads', 'yes'], ['No', 'no'], ['No', 'no']],
  ['Avatar group', ['Built in, with a +N chip', 'yes'], ['No', 'no'], ['No', 'no']],
  ['Initials', ['Yes, with letters that clear WCAG AA', 'yes'], ['Yes, an initials style', 'yes'], ['No', 'no']],
  ['Theming', 'CSS variables, so dark mode needs no re-render', 'Options per style, such as backgroundColor', 'A colors prop with your palette'],
  ['Same seed, same image', 'Byte-identical SVG for all of 1.x, checked by checksums in CI', 'Seeded and deterministic', 'Deterministic from the name'],
  ['License', ['MIT for code and art', 'yes'], 'Code MIT; art licensed per style, some need attribution', 'MIT'],
]
const cell = (c: Cell) => (typeof c === 'string' ? { text: c, tone: '' } : { text: c[0], tone: c[1] })
---

<Base page="compare" title="Opaline vs DiceBear vs Boring Avatars" description="How Opaline compares with DiceBear and Boring Avatars: styles, frameworks, output formats, licensing and stability.">
  <div class="wrap page-head">
    <div>
      <h1>Opaline, DiceBear and Boring Avatars</h1>
      <p>All three turn a string into an avatar. They differ in what the avatars look like, where they run and what they promise. Here is an honest side-by-side, so you can pick the right one, even when it is not us.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="block flush">
    <div class="wrap docs-stack">
      <div class="card table-wrap">
        <table class="compare-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Opaline</th><th scope="col">DiceBear</th><th scope="col">Boring Avatars</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {ROWS.map(([label, ...cells]) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{label}</th>
                {cells.map((c) => {
                  const { text, tone } = cell(c)
                  return <td class={tone || undefined}>{text}</td>
                })}
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div>
        <div class="sec-head"><h2>Which one fits</h2></div>
        <div class="grid2">
          <div class="card pick">
            <h3>Pick DiceBear</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>You want lots of art styles to choose from.</li>
              <li>You want an image URL from an HTTP API, with no code at all.</li>
              <li>You need PNG or JPEG files made on the server.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="card pick">
            <h3>Pick Boring Avatars</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Your app is React only.</li>
              <li>You want minimal abstract shapes in your exact brand palette.</li>
              <li>You do not need photos, initials or groups.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="card pick">
            <h3>Pick Opaline</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>You want friendly faces or readable initials, not abstract shapes.</li>
              <li>You ship more than one framework, or server-render and need no hydration warnings.</li>
              <li>You want photos with a fallback, avatar groups and PNG export built in.</li>
              <li>You cache avatars and need them never to change within a major version.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <div class="sec-head">
          <h2>Switching over</h2>
          <p>The seed is the only thing you need to keep. Pass the same user ID, email or name you pass today.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="grid2">
          <div class="card pkg">
            <b>From DiceBear</b>
            <pre>{`// before
import { createAvatar } from '@dicebear/core'
import { lorelei } from '@dicebear/collection'
createAvatar(lorelei, { seed: user.id }).toString()

// after
import { createAvatar } from '@inlaylab/opaline'
import { clay } from '@inlaylab/opaline/clay'
createAvatar(clay, { seed: user.id }).svg`}</pre>
          </div>
          <div class="card pkg">
            <b>From Boring Avatars</b>
            <pre>{`// before
import Avatar from 'boring-avatars'
<Avatar name={user.name} variant="marble" />

// after
import { Avatar } from '@inlaylab/opaline-react'
import { aura } from '@inlaylab/opaline/aura'
<Avatar seed={user.name} look={aura} />`}</pre>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="fine-print">
        Checked in November 2026 against each project's npm package and README (@dicebear/core 10.7, @dicebear/collection 9.4, boring-avatars 2.0). Both are good open-source projects; this page shows none of their art. If something here is out of date, <a href="https://github.com/Inlaylab/Opaline/issues">open an issue</a> and we will fix it. <a href={`${BASE}playground/`}>Try Opaline in the playground</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
